What is driving the semiconductor foundry service market?
Advanced computing demand, process-node transitions and the desire for geographically resilient supply are the central forces. Customers are not merely purchasing machine time. They contract for a qualified manufacturing process, yield learning, engineering support and an output schedule that fits the economics of their finished chip.

TSMC's 2026 results demonstrate leading-edge demand
On October 15, 2026, TSMC reported third-quarter revenue of US$46.72 billion, an increase of 41.2% year over year in U.S.-dollar terms. The company said advanced technologies at seven nanometers and below accounted for 79% of wafer revenue during that quarter. It also reported that two-nanometer technology represented 8% of wafer revenue, while three-nanometer contributed 33%.

These figures are company-specific financial and technology disclosures, not a measurement of worldwide foundry services. They nevertheless show why customers with high-performance designs need access to process technologies capable of producing leading-edge chips in volume. A process announced as available is not the same as a customer's design achieving acceptable yield, however. Product designers must qualify libraries, verify power and timing, prepare masks and test production output before contracted capacity becomes a reliable commercial supply.

A smaller node may command higher fabrication prices while older processes remain essential; revenue growth and physical wafer starts therefore need not move together.

