Wafer diameter affects manufacturing economics and tool compatibility. Application class captures different performance, longevity and cost requirements, while customer type indicates who owns the design and purchases manufacturing services. Technology node captures an important part of process differentiation but does not, by itself, measure reliability, radio-frequency performance or suitability for power devices.

The distinctions have immediate implications. A fabless accelerator designer evaluating a sub-seven-nanometer process has different constraints from an automotive supplier needing an established analog process with long-term support. The complete semiconductor foundry service report helps compare these categories without combining advanced-node revenue and mature-process wafer counts into a misleading single metric.

